{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>Now I know, etc.</b> The meaning is that although the nations have great and mighty princes to do battle on their behalf and to bring harm upon their enemies — and whoever raises a hand against them, even so, they do not direct their vengeance against the opposing nation in measured correspondence to the rebellion, unlike the Elohim of Israel, who directs the punishment to measure with the very same measure of the rebel and the evildoer — sinking them as they sought to sink [others], etc. Also, with regard to the evil event [i.e., the plague], one may explain that it did not come merely as a blow against the one struck; not so the Elohim of Israel, for all the plagues and afflictions with which He chastised Pharaoh were precisely directed toward what they had schemed against [Israel]. Go out and learn from what they said, of blessed memory (Tanchuma), regarding the intention behind the plagues, that each one corresponded to a particular evil measure that the Egyptians had schemed, etc."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:29:33.942676806Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}